clean-arch/PRD.md:724:- background the analyst already knew, such as the company's history ("the company was founded in 1998"); an arrangement the item only describes as in place, even when no date is given: customers or partners that use, host, run or have built on the company's products, and the benefits they describe from that use ("two airlines fly the planemaker's jets"; "three insurers run claims on the company's software and report faster payouts"); a partnership, contract, plant or investment the item itself dates to an earlier month or year with no new step ("earlier this year the company partnered with the glassmaker on three plants"; "in May it signed a $3 billion cloud contract"); it does not cover a step the item reports as news, taken, decided, planned or under way (an order, a selection, an integration, an expansion, a deployment), even when it gives no date ("the retailer's largest supplier is opening its own stores, people familiar said this week"; "the two largest cement makers are adding kilns to meet demand"), nor a customer's, supplier's or partner's newly disclosed revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amount; a deal the item mentions as already existing is an arrangement in place even with its amount ("a rival airline already leases 40 of the same jets");
clean-arch/PRD.md:1056:Alex approved returning the evidence tier, original statement date and described period in Step 1. The draft also identifies the original party and reporting speaker separately. Mapping these fields to the existing writer and readers remains to be verified, including how source-specific attribution and tier are retained when a repeat adds another source. The signed historical standard uses more specific opening categories and check-date rules; Alex has clarified that the clean design may open a meaningful topic without a known decision date when later observable developments can help assess it. Other differences remain subject to audit. This draft must not be described as fully compatible with that standard or the production schema without a separate review.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1060:This section and the two after it describe the earlier Luna draft. They are not c8.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1256:A thesis can matter to several companies or assets. A supplier disruption may be relevant to its customers and suppliers; a macro thesis may be relevant to many entities for different reasons. If the system makes separate copies of the same thesis and its evidence for every entity, keeping those copies consistent becomes difficult. A possible direction is to keep a shared thesis and evidence history and link it to each relevant entity with an explanation of the connection. How this fits the existing thesis and evidence database remains to be designed.
clean-arch/PRD.md:1270:This is a hypothetical example, not a statement about actual companies. Suppose a source reports a disruption at Foxconn, and a previously established relationship indicates that Foxconn supplies Apple. The system could maintain one disruption thesis and its evidence, then identify Apple as potentially relevant through the supplier relationship. If later evidence changes the assessment, or the supplier relationship is found to have ended before the disruption, the affected connection would need to be reconsidered. The original source and the history of how the connection was reached should remain understandable. The exact reassessment and update mechanism is not yet designed.

